nodecode-deepseek-search is a rewrite of JasonSwindle's JasonSwindle/hermes-deepseek-search-fetch:

  https://github.com/JasonSwindle/hermes-deepseek-search-fetch
  commit 95738a9913ac6595b73e655fad1d79414b1311dc
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Jason Swindle (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is the repository root of that commit: one Python module
that registered a web provider with a harness, answering that harness's
search and extract calls from POST /v1/tools/search and /v1/tools/fetch.
What is carried over is everything between the two tools and the vendor: the
two endpoints, the bearer key and where it comes from, the 128-character
query clamp at a word boundary, the reversal of the fetch endpoint's
backslash escaping in both content fields, the search fields (query, count,
need_content, localization), the hit signals (rerank_score, authority, date,
site_name), the retries on 408, 429 and 5xx with Retry-After when the call
can afford it, the 402 top-up sentence, the 300-character cap on vendor
error text, and the rule that a failed answer is a line the model can repair
from rather than an exception. It was rewritten in Common Lisp against
Nodecode's cell contract, as two model-facing tools (deepseek_search,
deepseek_fetch) and two eval verbs, since Nodecode has no web-backend seam
for a cell to plug into.

What was left out, and why: the harness-shaped parts -- the provider base
class, the settings surface, the batch deadline that kept a multi-URL
extract inside that harness's own timeout (here each request has its own
ceiling), and the fallback to the harness's keyless search ring, which is
the host's behaviour and not the plugin's. The site_icon url each hit can
carry is not printed, because a model reading a text answer cannot use an
icon. The Python test suite, its live acceptance script and its dev-only
pinned requirements are not carried; this folder has its own offline tests,
every vendor call stubbed.
